́nez-Vázquez and R. J. Cross, J. Chem. Phys. 104, 5589 (1996); D. Yin and A. D. MacKerell, J. Comp. Chem. 19, 334 (1998); S. Maruyama and T. Kimura, Proc. ASME Heat Transfer Division 2, 405 (2000)."}]}, {"reference": {"imprint": {"publisher": "Wiley"}, "label": "21", "publication_info": {"year": 1954}, "misc": ["Molecular Theory of Gases and Liquids (John", "& Sons, New York,)"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Hirschfelder, J.O."}, {"full_name": "Curtiss, C.F."}, {"full_name": "Bird, R.B."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[21] J. O. Hirschfelder, C. F. Curtiss, and R. B. Bird, Molecular Theory of Gases and Liquids (John Wiley & Sons, New York, 1954)."}]}, {"reference": {"urls": [{"value": "http://www.zyvex.com/nanotech/feynman.html"}], "label": "22", "publication_info": {"year": 1959}, "misc": ["APS meeting, Caltech, Pasadena, see"], "authors": [{"full_name": "Feynman, R."}]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[22] R. Feynman (1959), APS meeting, Caltech, Pasadena, see http://www.zyvex.com/nanotech/feynman.html."}]}, {"reference": {"label": "23", "misc": ["The high-vacuum visibility did not reach its optimal value in all of our measurements, although it stayed significantly above the classical value. We checked that the observed p0 was independent of the attainable maximum visibility, which is consistent with attributing the visibility loss to vibrational noise"]}, "raw_refs": [{"schema": "text", "source": "arXiv", "value": "[23] The high-vacuum visibility did not reach its optimal value in all of our measurements, although it stayed significantly above the classical value. We checked that the observed p0 was independent of the attainable maximum visibility, which is consistent with attributing the visibility loss to vibrational noise."}]}], "number_of_pages": 4, "referenced_authors_bais": ["S.Haroche.1", "T.Weber.6", "S.Durr.3", "W.M.Itano.2", "D.J.Wineland.1", "J.Petschinka.1", "G.Rempe.1", "J.M.Raimond.1", "T.Nonn.1", "L.Hackermuller.2", "Q.A.Turchette.1", "M.Brune.1", "E.Joos.1", "S.Uttenthaler.2", "E.Hagley.1", "B.G.Englert.1", "B.E.King.1", "X.Maitre.1", "C.Wunderlich.2", "G.N.Fleming.1", "H.D.Zeh.1", "A.Maali.1", "B.Brezger.1", "C.A.Sackett.1", "M.Arndt.1", "G.C.Ghirardi.1", "A.Rimini.2", "C.Monroe.2", "M.Tegmark.1", "H.Walther.3", "R.Penrose.1", "A.Zeilinger.1", "M.R.Gallis.2", "J.Dreyer.1", "D.Kielpinski.2", "C.J.Myatt.1", "M.O.Scully.1"], "figures": [{"filename": "fig1.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Schematic setup of the near-field interferometer for C$_{70}$ fullerenes. The third grating uncovers the interference pattern by yielding an oscillatory transmission with lateral shift $x_{\\rm s}$. Collisions with gas molecules localize the molecular center-of-mass wave function leading to a reduced visibility of the interference pattern.", "label": "fig:sketch", "source": "arxiv", "key": "f2283a3de51c996cb902da76e2545a6b", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/f2283a3de51c996cb902da76e2545a6b"}, {"filename": "fig2.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Fullerene fringe visibility vs.~methane  gas pressure on a semi-logarithmic scale. The exponential decay indicates that each collision leads to a complete loss of coherence. The solid line gives the prediction of decoherence theory, see text. The inset shows the observed interference pattern at (a) $p=0.05\\times 10^{-6}\\,$mbar and (b) $p=0.6\\times 10^{-6}\\,$mbar.", "label": "fig:vp", "source": "arxiv", "key": "7a9cb5107ef5e076237422ea540286cb", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/7a9cb5107ef5e076237422ea540286cb"}, {"filename": "fig3.png", "material": "preprint", "caption": "Experimental decoherence pressure $\\pv$ for various gases compared to the predictions of decoherence theory.", "label": "fig:druckfig", "source": "arxiv", "key": "1d8a2031fcae8d5e5050fea141bcd57b", "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/1d8a2031fcae8d5e5050fea141bcd57b"}], "inspire_categories": [{"term": "Quantum Physics", "source": "arxiv"}], "preprint_date": "2003-03-14", "author_count": 6, "first_author": {"full_name": "Hornberger, Klaus", "last_name": "Hornberger", "first_name": "Klaus", "recid": 1073918}, "public_notes": [{"source": "arXiv", "value": "4 pages, 3 figures"}], "control_number": 2990696, "dois": [{"material": "publication", "source": "arXiv", "value": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.90.160401"}], "earliest_date": "2003-03-14", "document_type": ["article"], "texkeys": ["Hornberger:2003wik"], "abstracts": [{"source": "arXiv", "value": "We study the loss of spatial coherence in the extended wave function of fullerenes due to collisions with background gases. From the gradual suppression of quantum interference with increasing gas pressure we are able to support quantitatively both the predictions of decoherence theory and our picture of the interaction process. We thus explore the practical limits of matter wave interferometry at finite gas pressures and estimate the required experimental vacuum conditions for interferometry with even larger objects.", "abstract_source_suggest": {"input": "arXiv"}}], "primary_arxiv_category": ["quant-ph"], "titles": [{"source": "arXiv", "title": "Collisional decoherence observed in matter wave interferometry"}], "facet_author_name": ["2720093_Lucia Hackermueller", "2258318_Stefan Uttenthaler", "1073918_Klaus Hornberger", "1073935_Markus Arndt", "1693289_Anton Zeilinger", "2977836_Bjoern Brezger"], "_oai": {"sets": ["Literature"], "id": "oai:inspirehep.net:2990696", "updated": "2026-02-09T15:02:56.795682"}, "curated": false, "journal_title_variants": ["Phys.Rev.Lett.", "Phys. Rev. Lett."], "arxiv_eprints": [{"categories": ["quant-ph"], "value": "quant-ph/0303093"}]}}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.90.160401&size=5&page=1", "bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=doi%3A10.1103%2Fphysrevlett.90.160401&size=5&page=1&format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/lit